## Observations
1. **Public notebook scores do not show code strength.**
   - The current search score tops out at 2625 (Ozer V38). The LB top was about 3063 on 2026-09-23.
   - Best-version scores above 2700 come from versions that ran earlier: August, or Sept 9–20.
   - Re-running the same code later scores much lower. Examples:
     - Metav4 v13, published as top-2-master-engine-v4: v4 2784.6, current v11 2035.5.
     - flexonafft: v59 2767.3 (Aug 14), current v103 787.7.
     - master-engine-v3: v3 2712.8, current v25 2041.0.
   - Treat these numbers as ladder timing, not strength.
2. **Almost everything public now is one code family.**
   - 26 of the 32 unique agents carry the Horizon / Ozer shop-router tape chassis. The markers are `DEFAULT_SETTINGS`, `make_agent`, `_R37_HORIZONS` and the embedded `actions.json` tapes.
   - Upstream chain: thomastschinkel runtime → yhay81 shop-router-0908/0909 tapes and 13 shop-pair routes → Ozer V25…V57. prvsiyan, aurax7, Dmitrii Gluzdov, Rayk Kretzschmar and jaxa623 contributed layers along the way.
   - Many "new" notebooks are byte-identical reposts:
     - Metav4 v13 appears 3 times: top-2-master-engine-v4, autonomous-ai-farming-agent, a-song-of-ice-and-fire v6.
     - Ozer V43 appears twice: arsgorynich v43-local-confirmed, degnonguidi utils-v1.
     - Ozer V45 is reposted as degnonguidi cloning-agent.
     - Ozer V39 is reposted as master-engine-v53e01d74d8f.
3. **None of the public agents beats our v5_market locally (0/64 games).**
   - The closest are the Pipe-16 HybridOpening + Metav4 line: harvest-ledger V54 and farmer-john-idle-seller (−1.7k / −1.3k), Metav4 v13 (−1.8k / −1.3k), nihilistic V57 (−2.3k / −1.2k), Ozer V53 (−1.3k / −1.5k) and Ozer V55 (−2.0k / −1.5k).
   - The Ozer V31–V48 chassis variants (incl. jaxa623, aurax7, tetsutani, pipe-5, game-theoretic) trail by 2.4–9.6k per game.
   - August route-memory tapes and open-loop episode traces trail by 29–72k: v21, v21.1, BL-MDgogo, flexonafft, lynnsakurai lean, Rayk THUNDER trace, prvsiyan moon.
4. **Herd sizes:**
   - The Horizon family holds 3–4 geese, 7–8 cows and 6 sheep. The Metav4 / Pipe-16 line has 2 geese by day 9 and 4 geese in total. Ozer V39/V48, tetsutani and aurax7 reach 2 geese only by day 11 and keep 3.
   - The independent lines (flexonafft, moon, v21, lean 8C4S) keep **no geese** at all.
   - No public agent runs the ~6-geese-by-day-11 programme that `adil-board/research/gap_vs_top.md` measured for top-20 ladder teams.
5. **Land and hires are identical across the family, so they don't separate these agents.**
   - Quadrant 2 is bought on day 6 and quadrant 3 on day 11. A 4th quadrant (day 18) is bought in only one of the two worlds.
   - About 275 HIRE orders per game, about 9 hands mid-day, 12–13 at peak.
   - The independents buy land on day 6/7 + 10 and never a 4th quadrant.
6. **Where the money gap opens:** the family runs almost identically to v5_market through day 16 (gap under ~$350, except Ozer V39 at about −$1.1k and the tape replayer master-engine-v3 at −$1.7k on day 11). The deficit builds on days 17–29. The differentiators are therefore late-game market mechanics:
   - premium-sale reservation horizon: Metav4 RACE from step 192 with horizon 40, V55 uses 41, V57 uses 48;
   - same-turn sale races against clones (V44 race, jaxa623 front-loading and 2-turn advance, tetsutani 3-tick ADV);
   - queue cleanup (V48).
   Production is not the differentiator.
7. **Opening-market micro-tricks:** every family agent sells from turn 0.
   - Ozer V45 does a 70-wheat buy-and-sell round trip in one order.
   - jaxa623 K0006 does a single-order step-0 round trip.
   - Ozer V53 detects the rival's opening from its step-2 wheat inventory.
   - Wheat bought as feed is 125–160 units per game (v5 ~125). moon buys about 970 because it has no wheat plan.
8. **Newest public ideas (Sept 20–28) are small labour tweaks:**
   - "Productive idle workers": in the Pipe-16 line (V54 harvest-ledger, V55) and Gluzdov's "A Smaller Market Shock" (leoprovorov 3141…), idle hands grow one temporary wheat crop on days 0–2 before the tile becomes pasture. The day-6 bank differs from v5_market by only −$8…+$52 for these agents.
   - Shop-unlock prediction overlay: god-s-mode.
   - Neither changes the mid-game economy.
9. **Shop routing** in every family agent is a lookup keyed by the unlocked shop pair: `_R108_SHOP_ROUTES`, or the 15-entry `SHOP_PLANS` table in master-engine-v3. The same 5 MB `actions.json` tape bank is shared by master-engine-v3 and kaggricult-man.
10. **Runtime:** the worst per-turn time is 639 ms (Ozer V53, which runs an opponent-sale forecaster). The Metav4-family agents run at 200–400 ms, and the small tape agents at 11–100 ms. None timed out.
# Non-RL discussion digest — T004

Read through the read-only Kaggle CLI on 2026-09-24. These are decision rules
for Horizon experiments, not evidence that a particular control change works.

| Source | Finding | Concrete use here |
|---|---|---|
| [#742856](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/kaggriculture/discussion/742856), “What actually predicted the ladder” | A passive or frozen opponent creates a different market. The author found local margin correlations close to zero until using strong trading opponents and ladder-derived seeds. They also report that near the top many wins are separated by under $1k; paired win rate and per-opponent results were more informative than aggregate bank. | T012 must retain M&M&P&Q tapes as diagnostics but use v5/v4, Parthenos and Tschinkel as decisive opponents. Report paired both seats, win rate and per-opponent breakdown; do not select a production overlay from cash alone. |
| [#742856](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/kaggriculture/discussion/742856) | Fixed seed + fixed agents reproduce ladder outcomes under 1.32.7. The post also warns that agents silently returning PASS can look like a low-bank result instead of a crash. | Keep the full-state gpuenv parity gate and make a starting-money bank a failure signal. The T012 overlay needs replay attribution of completed PLANT/FERTILIZE actions and final unused inputs before its score is interpreted. |
| [#734000](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/kaggriculture/discussion/734000), “How path dependent…” | Reports very different transient ratings for byte-identical submissions; commenters describe early matchmaking and seed effects. | Do not recommend replacing either active v5 submission from a short rating swing. Use local paired evidence; the human, not agents, decides any submission. |
| [#731215](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/kaggriculture/discussion/731215), Daily Top Episodes Dataset | Daily index prioritizes high average-rating games but has a fixed 20GB/day cap; replay count is affected by file size. | Use it for candidate replay discovery and macro statistics, then re-simulate selected episodes. Do not treat its raw episode distribution as an unbiased policy-training set. |

## Immediate tests

1. For every market-only T012 overlay, replay at least one firing game and
   record successful `PLANT STRAWBERRY`, `FERTILIZE`, final seed/fertilizer
   stock, and callback time. Inputs without matching unit work are a loss,
   not an intervention.
2. Gate only a passing, attributed overlay on the diverse paired suite. A
   tape-only improvement is insufficient because tapes are brittle on foreign
   seeds.
3. Keep the two current ladder agents until a broad local comparison supports
   a replacement and the human chooses to submit it.
# T010: RL lessons applied to Kaggriculture

Retrieved 2026-09-24 through the read-only Kaggle CLI. Sources below are
competition discussion topic IDs; no submissions were made.

## Evidence

| Source | What it establishes | Applicability |
|---|---|---|
| Orbit Wars [#724268](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/orbit-wars/discussion/724268), 1st place | PPO self-play eventually won with a 200M transformer and 15B steps, but required 2,400 B200-hours. The author first changed the action from raw angle to target selection; replay parity and a rewritten fast environment were prerequisite. They later wished for checkpoint-league play. | The compute scale rules out raw-action-from-scratch as the main seven-day bet. Environment parity and a smaller action abstraction transfer directly. |
| Orbit Wars [#723728](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/orbit-wars/discussion/723728), 2nd place | BC reached top 10, RL finetuning top 5. The final policy reduced each entity to no-op/all-in and short-range target selection, then used 10B self-play steps. | A demonstrator warm-up is useful, but full imitation alone is insufficient. We should accept top-replay BC only for the macro control layer. |
| Orbit Wars [#697725](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/orbit-wars/discussion/697725) | Fast environment before architecture search; add one architecture delta at a time; feature/action inductive bias matters below massive scale; growing PPO clip fraction warns before instability. | Run small, observable residual experiments, with KL/clip-fraction checks and fixed baselines. |
| Kaggriculture [#741743](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/kaggriculture/discussion/741743) | Silver-zone PPO used **macro** decisions, BC warm-up, then RL; author reports roughly 300k games. Higher-scoring replay demos helped more. Bank difference was informative early, then final win/loss was added. | Direct support for residual macro control and requesting top-replay macro demonstrations from T011. |
| Kaggriculture [#738619](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/kaggriculture/discussion/738619) | Full-action PPO at ~10k steps/s reached 80k terminal cash then saturated. A 50/25/25 self-play/active/banked pool and BC traces helped; product-level shaping created a melon farm. The author later moved to an executor-plus-intent hybrid. | Reject raw action space. Use terminal margin first; do not reward product activity. Add fixed external opponents before interpreting self-play gains. |
| Kaggriculture [#742246](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/kaggriculture/discussion/742246) | Exact routing, legality and marginal-price arithmetic belong in the executor; learned policy should own portfolio, land and liquidation decisions. Fixed strong external opponents stop self-play from masking regressions. | Matches Horizon executor plus a daily residual control vector. |
| Kaggriculture [#741730](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/kaggriculture/discussion/741730), [#742737](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/kaggriculture/discussion/742737) | Static-opponent training regressed; self-play was weak against public agents. Reported training ranges are many millions of steps / days on stronger GPUs. | Do not interpret a training return as progress. Use short smoke runs only to verify learning mechanics, followed by a paired external gate. |

## Decision and falsifiable first experiment

Train a small PPO policy once per game day, emitting a bounded residual vector
over Horizon's economic controls. `u=0` must reproduce the Horizon baseline
exactly. The executor keeps legal unit routing and market mechanics. Observation
should include own and opponent portfolio, market inventory/price, unlocked-shop
counts and calendar; training reward is terminal money margin, scaled but not
activity-shaped.

First smoke gate: policy rollout, value loss, KL, entropy and clip fraction must
remain finite; a zero residual rollout must equal Horizon's result for fixed
seeds. A later candidate gate is paired both seats against Horizon v4/v5 and
non-Horizon opponents. If its confidence interval includes no effect, it is not
promoted.

